1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,496
Here's a genuinely uncomfortable fact this section has been building toward.

2
00:00:04,496 --> 00:00:08,860
At the budget levels most testing actually runs at, twenty-five dollars a day, a

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:13,224
handful of conversions, the difference between what looks like a winner and what

4
00:00:13,224 --> 00:00:15,351
looks like a loser is often just noise.

5
00:00:15,351 --> 00:00:17,315
Random variation, not a real signal.

6
00:00:17,315 --> 00:00:19,606
That's not a reason to give up on testing.

7
00:00:19,606 --> 00:00:24,079
It's a reason to read results with the same honesty this course has asked of every

8
00:00:24,079 --> 00:00:26,698
claim, every generated image, every avatar clip.

9
00:00:26,698 --> 00:00:31,062
A number that looks like a win can still be lying to you, and the fix isn't more

10
00:00:31,062 --> 00:00:31,389
taste.

11
00:00:31,389 --> 00:00:34,935
It's knowing what a small sample can and can't actually tell you.

12
00:00:34,935 --> 00:00:38,263
This connects straight back to the course's opening argument.

13
00:00:38,263 --> 00:00:42,354
"Winners are found, not picked" only holds if the finding itself is honest.

14
00:00:42,354 --> 00:00:45,572
A winner declared from three lucky conversions isn't found.

15
00:00:45,572 --> 00:00:49,936
It's picked, dressed up in a percentage sign to look more rigorous than a guess.

16
00:00:49,936 --> 00:00:52,391
Notice how quietly that substitution happens.

17
00:00:52,391 --> 00:00:54,300
Nobody sets out to fool themselves.

18
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:58,774
It just feels like reading a dashboard, not like making a judgement call, which is

19
00:00:58,774 --> 00:01:03,356
exactly why the habit of asking "is this actually enough data" has to be deliberate.

20
00:01:03,356 --> 00:01:04,829
It won't happen on its own.

21
00:01:05,379 --> 00:01:07,976
Same ratio, roughly three to one, in both rows.

22
00:01:07,976 --> 00:01:10,297
Wildly different confidence in the answer.

23
00:01:10,297 --> 00:01:14,939
Three conversions against one is the kind of gap a single lucky click can produce by

24
00:01:14,939 --> 00:01:15,602
pure chance.

25
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:20,244
Thirty against ten, at ten times the volume, is a gap that chance alone struggles to

26
00:01:20,244 --> 00:01:20,687
explain.

27
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:24,997
This is the part that catches people who are otherwise doing everything right.

28
00:01:24,997 --> 00:01:29,252
The instinct to call a winner the moment one ad set pulls ahead is completely

29
00:01:29,252 --> 00:01:33,618
natural, and it's exactly the instinct this lecture is asking you to slow down.

30
00:01:33,618 --> 00:01:35,994
Why does volume fix what ratio alone can't?

31
00:01:35,994 --> 00:01:40,471
Because a single lucky click, one person who happened to convert for reasons that

32
00:01:40,471 --> 00:01:44,505
had nothing to do with your creative, can swing a three-conversion sample

33
00:01:44,505 --> 00:01:45,223
dramatically.

34
00:01:45,223 --> 00:01:48,981
The same lucky click barely moves a thirty-conversion sample at all.

35
00:01:48,981 --> 00:01:51,413
More data doesn't make your creative better.

36
00:01:51,413 --> 00:01:55,834
It makes any one stray result matter less, which is exactly what you want from a

37
00:01:55,834 --> 00:01:56,497
measurement.

38
00:01:56,497 --> 00:01:58,266
Notice this cuts both ways, too.

39
00:01:58,266 --> 00:02:02,908
Small samples produce noisy results in both directions, not just disappointing ones.

40
00:02:02,908 --> 00:02:05,947
A row-one gap tempts you to declare a winner too early.

41
00:02:05,947 --> 00:02:10,147
A row-one loss tempts you to kill an angle that might genuinely have worked.

42
00:02:10,147 --> 00:02:14,679
The discipline is identical either way: wait for the volume the top row never had.

43
00:02:15,229 --> 00:02:19,579
Three honest questions, and notice none of them require a statistics degree to

44
00:02:19,579 --> 00:02:19,970
answer.

45
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:22,926
Single-digit conversions mean treat a lead as a hint.

46
00:02:22,926 --> 00:02:27,499
If flipping the outcome of one or two individual conversions would change which ad

47
00:02:27,499 --> 00:02:32,017
set looks like the winner, the result hasn't earned your confidence yet, whatever

48
00:02:32,017 --> 00:02:34,248
the dashboard's percentage sign implies.

49
00:02:34,248 --> 00:02:38,487
That third question matters because the kill rule and a real winner call are

50
00:02:38,487 --> 00:02:39,324
different bars.

51
00:02:39,324 --> 00:02:42,559
Clearing the kill rule means an ad set isn't clearly dead.

52
00:02:42,559 --> 00:02:45,236
It doesn't automatically mean it's the best one.

53
00:02:45,236 --> 00:02:49,754
Give genuine contenders more spend before you crown one, not just enough to avoid

54
00:02:49,754 --> 00:02:50,368
killing it.

55
00:02:50,368 --> 00:02:54,886
Run this same check against the losing ad sets too, not just the apparent leader.

56
00:02:54,886 --> 00:02:59,571
An ad set at zero conversions after a small amount of spend is a genuinely different

57
00:02:59,571 --> 00:03:03,866
situation from one at zero after clearing Section 7.2's full kill-rule spend.

58
00:03:03,866 --> 00:03:06,487
The first is still too early to judge honestly.

59
00:03:06,487 --> 00:03:08,439
The second already has your answer.

60
00:03:08,439 --> 00:03:12,455
Notice these three questions don't ask you to wait indefinitely, either.

61
00:03:12,455 --> 00:03:16,973
Section 7.2's kill rule already sets a floor, and once you're well past it with a

62
00:03:16,973 --> 00:03:21,658
genuine gap that survives the swap test, that's a real result, not an excuse to keep

63
00:03:21,658 --> 00:03:23,387
testing forever out of caution.

64
00:03:23,387 --> 00:03:26,065
The goal is honesty about timing, not paralysis.

65
00:03:26,615 --> 00:03:30,813
One honest thing to say plainly, because it would be easy to promise otherwise.

66
00:03:30,813 --> 00:03:35,171
There's no formula in this lecture that turns three conversions into a trustworthy

67
00:03:35,171 --> 00:03:36,819
result, because no formula can.

68
00:03:36,819 --> 00:03:40,805
Statistical tools can tell you how confident to be given the data you have.

69
00:03:40,805 --> 00:03:43,090
They can't manufacture data you don't have.

70
00:03:43,090 --> 00:03:45,588
The actual discipline here isn't a calculation.

71
00:03:45,588 --> 00:03:49,893
It's the willingness to say "I don't know yet" instead of forcing an early result

72
00:03:49,893 --> 00:03:51,647
into a decision it can't support.

73
00:03:51,647 --> 00:03:53,826
Why does this feel harder than it should?

74
00:03:53,826 --> 00:03:58,237
Because "I don't know yet" is an uncomfortable thing to report to a boss, a client,

75
00:03:58,237 --> 00:04:00,841
or yourself, when everyone wants an answer today.

76
00:04:00,841 --> 00:04:05,040
Saying it anyway, and letting a promising ad set run for real volume before you

77
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:09,345
commit to it, is the actual professional move here, even though it feels like the

78
00:04:09,345 --> 00:04:11,099
less confident one in the moment.

79
00:04:11,099 --> 00:04:15,297
This is exactly the credibility argument the whole course has been making about

80
00:04:15,297 --> 00:04:16,573
honesty since Section 1.

81
00:04:16,573 --> 00:04:20,878
Naming a limit plainly, rather than papering over it with false precision, is the

82
00:04:20,878 --> 00:04:25,183
strongest signal you can give that the rest of what you're saying can be trusted.

83
00:04:25,183 --> 00:04:29,594
A vendor who tells you their tool always works is worth less than one who tells you

84
00:04:29,594 --> 00:04:30,923
exactly where it doesn't.

85
00:04:31,473 --> 00:04:36,097
Next lecture is the workshop where all of Section seven becomes your own account's

86
00:04:36,097 --> 00:04:40,721
actual structure: isolated ad sets, real budgets, a kill rule, and now the honesty

87
00:04:40,721 --> 00:04:43,428
to know when a result is actually ready to read.

88
00:04:43,428 --> 00:04:48,165
Before that, look back at the last result you called a winner, on any account you've

89
00:04:48,165 --> 00:04:48,391
run.

90
00:04:48,391 --> 00:04:51,549
Run this lecture's three questions against it, honestly.

91
00:04:51,549 --> 00:04:56,173
If it survives all three, that's a real result, and you can trust the decision you

92
00:04:56,173 --> 00:04:56,906
already made.

93
00:04:56,906 --> 00:05:00,346
If it doesn't, that's not a failure to correct retroactively.

94
00:05:00,346 --> 00:05:04,576
It's useful information about how much more carefully to read the next one.
